ADMS (https://www.w3.org/TR/vocab-adms/), the vocabulary for describing
interoperability assets, written into the same files as the DCAT record
(govuk.dcat=yes). It is for publications whose parts are not datasets: a logical
model, the data set specifications that conform to it, a code list. The publisher
declares each part an adms:Asset in the map (a data element named rdf:type), says
what kind it is with dct:type and what it conforms to or refers to, and a retired
part carries its status. A part is never made a dcat:Dataset by this table.

The DCAT table has already typed and described an asset; this table adds what is
ADMS's, as a second kind for the same record. dct:isPartOf is written only where
there is a catalogue to be part of. map= turns a value the harvest states
neutrally into the one the scheme uses. The table format is described in design 14,
section 7.
-->
<scheme id="adms" syntax="rdf">
  <prefix name="adms" uri="http://www.w3.org/ns/adms#"/>
  <prefix name="dct" uri="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"/>

  <kind record="asset" when="rdf:type = 'adms:Asset'">
    <property term="dct:type" as="dct:type" format="uri" node="yes" repeat="yes"/>
    <property term="adms:status" as="adms:status" format="uri" node="yes"
              map="retired=http://purl.org/adms/status/Withdrawn"/>
    <property term="dct:references" as="dct:references" format="uri" node="yes" repeat="yes"/>
  </kind>

  <!-- a member of a catalogue is part of it -->
  <kind record="asset" when="@members = 'many'">
    <relation name="isPartOf" target="publication" as="dct:isPartOf"/>
  </kind>
</scheme>
